WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:03.150
Gather up your rail guns and crank up
your angsty teenage energy,

00:00:03.166 --> 00:00:06.083
because they were covering something
brand new.

00:00:08.866 --> 00:00:11.966
I'm talking about Murder Drones,
one of the most popular animated series

00:00:11.966 --> 00:00:13.216
across all of YouTube,

00:00:13.216 --> 00:00:14.116
a series that has found

00:00:14.116 --> 00:00:17.900
a completely different original way
of communicating its story to us.

00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:21.150
Instead of using found footage
or hidden shorts videos to tell its story

00:00:21.166 --> 00:00:24.950
like most indie projects,
Murder Drones is releasing, get this, 20

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.650
minute episodes
one at a time in sequential order.

00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:29.416
What?

00:00:29.416 --> 00:00:32.716
No QR codes? No unlisted videos? Madness.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:36.050
Who knew that structuring your
animated series like an actual animated

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:38.300
series
would feel so refreshing these days?

00:00:39.766 --> 00:00:40.816
Hello internet!

00:00:40.816 --> 00:00:43.666
Welcome to Film Theory,
the show that locked itself

00:00:43.666 --> 00:00:47.666
behind three layers of doors to hide from
the watchful eye of the algorithm drones.

00:00:47.666 --> 00:00:48.600
And, ladies and gentlemen,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.316
today we're going to absolute solve
the world that is Murder Drones.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.650
Produced by Glitch Entertainment here
on YouTube, Murder Drones tells the story

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.916
of some teenage robots who have to save
the universe from a rogue A.I.

00:00:58.916 --> 00:00:59.600
program.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.400
And for as much as I was just praising

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.483
the series earlier for its straightforward
content delivery strategy,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.016
it doesn't mean that
the story is a purely linear experience.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:09.233
This show is filled with twists,
flashbacks

00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:11.300
and fourth wall breaks
that keep you on the edge of your seat,

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.016
guessing
about what's going to happen next.

00:01:13.016 --> 00:01:14.400
And that's not an exaggeration either.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.300
If you look away for 2 seconds,
you stand to miss a hidden detail

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.816
or clue buried in something
as simple as flashing source

00:01:19.816 --> 00:01:23.700
code or a class presentation,
which is where I come in today.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:26.250
Since this is a new series that I'd like
to talk about moving forward...

00:01:29.666 --> 00:01:32.633
Like I said, since this is a new show
that I'd like to talk about again

00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:34.366
sometime in the future,
I want to start by giving you

00:01:34.366 --> 00:01:37.766
all a simple, straightforward
overview of the plot and key details

00:01:37.766 --> 00:01:39.833
that’ll help you fill in the gaps
that you might have missed

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:42.300
during your first
or even your 40th watchthrough.

00:01:42.316 --> 00:01:45.616
I'm also going to be doing it
not in episode order but rather timeline

00:01:45.616 --> 00:01:48.833
order, the order that all the major events
actually happened in the universe

00:01:48.833 --> 00:01:49.366
since that's the way

00:01:49.366 --> 00:01:51.466
that makes the most sense,
as opposed to the twisty way

00:01:51.466 --> 00:01:53.183
it's ultimately presented in the show.

00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:55.100
And hey, if I make a mistake
along the way, just remember:

00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.066
Then by the end of

00:01:59.066 --> 00:02:02.400
the episode today, we’ll actually solve
the rest of the first season.

00:02:02.416 --> 00:02:04.516
That's right.
We are six episodes in right now,

00:02:04.516 --> 00:02:06.600
six episodes spread across two years.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.966
So why wait any longer?

00:02:07.966 --> 00:02:09.833
I suspect that
I know the big twist reveals

00:02:09.833 --> 00:02:10.500
that are going to be planned

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.583
for the final two episodes of the season
and potentially even deeper

00:02:13.583 --> 00:02:14.333
into the series.

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:15.866
And if you don't want to be spoiled, well,

00:02:15.866 --> 00:02:18.550
I suppose you could always
just forget everything that I say.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:24.416
So let's rewind,
or rather fast forward to the 3000s,

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.000
as I introduce you
to a potentially new series and solve

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.216
where the Murder Drones universe is headed
next.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.683
Get hyped guys.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.133
The main story of Murder Drones
takes place in the far

00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:40.516
future, the year 3071 to be exact,
based on these balloons

00:02:40.516 --> 00:02:42.916
that we see in the background of episode
3's prom scene.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.516
However, the real start to our story
happened quite a number of years earlier.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:49.616
This was a time when humans were expanding
beyond the confines of Earth

00:02:49.616 --> 00:02:50.966
to set up colonies on planets

00:02:50.966 --> 00:02:54.450
outside of our solar system,
mining them for all their resources.

00:02:54.466 --> 00:02:56.650
One of the biggest players in this world
was:

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:01.466
J.C. Jenson In Spaaaaaaaace.

00:03:01.466 --> 00:03:03.350
To help with both mining
and domestic life,

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:05.416
the company created
a series of autonomous A.I.

00:03:05.483 --> 00:03:07.133
robots called worker drones,

00:03:07.133 --> 00:03:10.100
each with their own personality,
thoughts and feelings. Basically,

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.983
these robots were indistinguishable
from a normal living creature.

00:03:13.016 --> 00:03:16.366
Sadly, though, not everyone agreed,
killing them for even perceived

00:03:16.366 --> 00:03:17.433
minor offenses.

00:03:23.483 --> 00:03:24.150
Pause it right there.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.800
Putting aside the obvious issues around
techno ethics here,

00:03:26.816 --> 00:03:30.766
that type of behavior right there is a big
no no in the world of murder drones.

00:03:30.766 --> 00:03:34.066
You see, all worker drones came packaged
with an established proper

00:03:34.066 --> 00:03:37.250
set of step by step instructions
for decommissioning old robots.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:40.000
And they're delivered to us on old VHS
tapes.

00:03:40.016 --> 00:03:41.850
Truly a film theorist classic.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.100
Why should you bother following
those instructions?

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:44.600
Well:

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.866
In short, if improperly

00:03:56.866 --> 00:04:01.433
disposed of, the dead robots might wake up
literally turning into a zombie drones.

00:04:01.433 --> 00:04:04.950
And not only do they wake up,
they're changed in that process.

00:04:04.966 --> 00:04:07.966
According to the VHS, these drones
experience, quote, potentially

00:04:07.966 --> 00:04:09.866
hazardous mutations to their coding,

00:04:09.866 --> 00:04:13.433
which we later learned to be known
as the Absolute Solver program.

00:04:13.433 --> 00:04:14.816
And that, my friends, is

00:04:14.816 --> 00:04:17.216
why you always get to take time
to read the terms of service.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:20.516
We're told that there's
only a 0.01% chance that the robots

00:04:20.516 --> 00:04:21.383
will come back to life.

00:04:21.383 --> 00:04:26.450
And of that 0.01%, only 0.7%
have this dangerous mutation, thereby

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:29.083
yielding a 0.007% chance

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.933
that the improperly disposed of drone
will have this Absolute Solver mutation.

00:04:32.966 --> 00:04:35.933
But, of course, this incredibly rare,
will-probably-never-happen

00:04:35.933 --> 00:04:38.116
thing, in fact, does happen.

00:04:38.116 --> 00:04:41.383
And it happens in the form of a worker
drone named Cyn,

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.966
waking up alone, confused
and scared in a pile of robot corpses.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:45.566
She's eventually

00:04:45.566 --> 00:04:47.066
found by a human girl named Tessa

00:04:47.066 --> 00:04:47.866
Elliott, who's taken

00:04:47.866 --> 00:04:50.866
a liking to some of the broken worker
drones and has been repurposing them

00:04:50.866 --> 00:04:51.716
to be her friends.

00:04:51.716 --> 00:04:55.883
Among the drones Tessa’s taken in
are the lovable goof N, the butt kicking V

00:04:55.883 --> 00:04:57.600
and the incredibly loyal J.

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:02.533
But clearly Cyn is different.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.950
The Absolute Solver program
that activated inside of her changed her.

00:05:05.966 --> 00:05:07.283
She speaks in a more robotic,

00:05:07.283 --> 00:05:11.333
Microsoft Sam-esque voice, literally
stating her emotions as they happen.

00:05:11.333 --> 00:05:14.766
And it generally seems that she's on the
fritz, acting like a puppet on strings.

00:05:18.466 --> 00:05:21.266
However, Tessa has a very different view
on the drones than her parents.

00:05:21.266 --> 00:05:25.283
Parents who, it would seem, work as higher
ups at the JC Jenson Corporation.

00:05:25.283 --> 00:05:28.316
While that's never directly stated
in the show, we can infer it from what's

00:05:28.316 --> 00:05:31.450
said during this moment
when Tessa gets locked up by her folks.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.950
Additionally, the family possesses
an entire staff of worker drones

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:39.950
at their mansion on earth,
treating them more like slaves and pests.

00:05:46.966 --> 00:05:51.316
Cyn, infected by the hazardous mutation
that is the Absolute Solver program, sees

00:05:51.316 --> 00:05:52.966
this and does not like it,

00:05:52.966 --> 00:05:54.683
eventually deciding
that it would be much better

00:05:54.683 --> 00:05:56.816
if these humans just
weren't around anymore.

00:05:56.816 --> 00:05:59.250
And so when the Elliotts
throw a big dinner party at their mansion,

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:02.866
Cyn executes her plan, transforming
into some eldritch abomination

00:06:02.866 --> 00:06:05.150
and killing everyone at the party
except for Tessa.

00:06:05.183 --> 00:06:08.333
The only human who decided
to treat this robot with respect.

00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:11.666
And Cyn didn't just stop at the party,
she killed everyone

00:06:11.666 --> 00:06:14.516
on the planet
by collapsing the core of earth.

00:06:20.633 --> 00:06:21.166
And if you thought

00:06:21.166 --> 00:06:24.200
killing off a planet's worth of creatures
would be enough, think again.

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:27.433
Remember, at this point in history,
humans have expanded far beyond just one

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:31.166
planet, meaning Cyn needed to find ways
of infiltrating these exoplanets

00:06:31.166 --> 00:06:34.166
if she truly wanted to wipe humanity
from existence.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:37.766
Thankfully for her, humans, ever
the curious bunch did most of the heavy

00:06:37.766 --> 00:06:38.666
lifting for her.

00:06:38.666 --> 00:06:41.966
After seeing what happened to Earth,
the humans brought Cyn's programing

00:06:41.966 --> 00:06:46.183
into their various systems to study it,
and that was a big mistake.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.933
The Absolute Solver program
does the exact same thing again on Copper

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:54.433
9, one of these human colonized exoplanets
and our main setting for the series.

00:06:54.466 --> 00:06:56.450
And again,
all human life gets extinguished.

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:57.983
The newly liberated worker drones

00:06:57.983 --> 00:06:59.866
now have no more commands
that they need to follow.

00:06:59.866 --> 00:07:02.100
They are free to live their lives
as they wish.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.183
And yet, this worker bot
peace doesn't last for very long.

00:07:05.183 --> 00:07:06.200
At some point in history

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:09.916
a new breed of drones,
the disassembly drones or murder drones,

00:07:09.916 --> 00:07:13.116
arrive on the scene with a directive
to eliminate all the workers.

00:07:13.133 --> 00:07:15.116
This forces
the everyday bots to live behind

00:07:15.116 --> 00:07:18.116
a series of three protective
walls, Attack On Titan style.

00:07:18.116 --> 00:07:19.700
And though the worker and murder drones

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:21.566
are told that this comes straight
from the J.C.

00:07:21.566 --> 00:07:25.200
Jenson Corporation, we learn in episode
six that that actually isn't the case.

00:07:25.216 --> 00:07:28.350
Instead, the murder drone massacres
that are happening across various planets

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:31.583
are being orchestrated
by Cyn for unknown reasons.

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:43.833
But that's weird, right?

00:07:43.833 --> 00:07:47.183
Cyn initially retaliated against humans
because of their callous mistreatment

00:07:47.183 --> 00:07:49.200
of the worker drones,
but now she's sending

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:52.966
murder bots out to massacre the very bots
that she initially worked to free?

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.616
Why? Well, I suspect it's for recruitment.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:57.500
I suspect it's a numbers game.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:58.666
If the odds of the Absolute

00:07:58.666 --> 00:08:02.450
Solver mutation activating in a dead
drone is 7 in 100,000, you’re

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:04.816
going to need to pile up
a whole lot of dead drones

00:08:04.816 --> 00:08:07.516
in order to get a couple
of Absolute Solvers out of the group.

00:08:07.516 --> 00:08:09.766
Cyn wants to kill more
and more worker drones,

00:08:09.766 --> 00:08:12.950
not because she's cruel,
but because she wants to build her army.

00:08:12.983 --> 00:08:13.883
What's more, the Absolute

00:08:13.883 --> 00:08:17.933
Solver mutation seems to get handed down
between generations of robot families,

00:08:17.933 --> 00:08:19.883
which, yes, is a real thing
in this universe.

00:08:19.883 --> 00:08:22.883
So Cyn may just be looking to speed up
natural selection of this

00:08:22.883 --> 00:08:26.450
super trait by weeding out all the worker
drones who don't possess it.

00:08:26.483 --> 00:08:30.050
This would basically be
the concept of robotic eugenics.

00:08:30.083 --> 00:08:32.483
Get ready to clip this next section
out for your MatPat

00:08:32.483 --> 00:08:34.266
out of context compilations, my friends.

00:08:34.266 --> 00:08:38.716
But you can't talk about Murder Drones
without talking about World War II.

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:41.716
During World War
II, the Nazis firmly believed in eugenics,

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:42.600
eliminating, quote

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:45.766
unquote, “undesirable” genetic elements
from the German population

00:08:45.766 --> 00:08:48.416
so that the “ideal” Aryan human
could dominate.

00:08:48.416 --> 00:08:51.783
In order to create a race of genetically
pure, physically fit human beings

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:53.333
they used tactics like forced

00:08:53.333 --> 00:08:56.366
sterilization and selective
breeding to ensure the, quote unquote,

00:08:56.366 --> 00:09:00.166
“positive genes” were the more prevalent
and circulated throughout the gene pool.

00:09:00.166 --> 00:09:03.716
By eliminating all the normal worker
drones, it enables the Absolute Solver

00:09:03.716 --> 00:09:07.016
code to dominate and procreate,
for lack of a better term.

00:09:07.016 --> 00:09:08.550
Beyond that, though, Cyn’s motives

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:10.883
remain unclear,
is it to build up enough of an army

00:09:10.883 --> 00:09:12.800
to fight
against the last strongholds of humanity?

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:14.866
Is it because she's lonely
and she wants more like her?

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:18.750
Is it because Absolute Solver has actually
been in control of her this entire time?

00:09:18.766 --> 00:09:19.800
Currently, it's unclear,

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:21.650
and we're just going to need more time
to see what happens.

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:25.516
Anyway, it's finally time that we cut
to the actual events of the show.

00:09:25.516 --> 00:09:26.450
Yeah, it is here

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:30.883
finally where we cut back to the story of
our characters as depicted in episode 1.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:34.016
Uzi, our main protagonist,
is one of the drones unknowingly affected

00:09:34.016 --> 00:09:37.166
with the Absolute Solver,
having inherited it from her mother, Nori.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:40.883
Sadly, Nori was killed by the murder
drones soon after Uzi’s birth.

00:09:40.883 --> 00:09:41.716
And now a teenager,

00:09:41.716 --> 00:09:42.800
Uzi is full of rebellious

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:45.866
teen spirit, annoyed that the workers
have hidden away from the murder drones

00:09:45.866 --> 00:09:47.250
rather than fighting them head on.

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.433
So armed with a homemade railgun,
she ventures

00:09:49.433 --> 00:09:52.666
out of the safety of the doors
and meets N, V and J,

00:09:52.733 --> 00:09:56.033
Tessa's drone army
that she rescued so many years ago.

00:09:56.066 --> 00:10:00.266
And now the murder drone squad tasked
with slaughtering the workers of Copper 9.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.316
Except there's one problem with this:

00:10:02.333 --> 00:10:05.400
by the end of the episode Uzi’s
Absolute Solver has activated.

00:10:14.416 --> 00:10:16.400
As we're getting this cliffhanger
ending, blink

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:19.800
and you'll miss an important line of code
flashing on Uzi’s eye visor.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:23.916
It says absoluteSolver string = FALSE
before quickly switching over to TRUE.

00:10:23.933 --> 00:10:27.833
Yep. Our main protagonist now
has herself a penchant for human genocide.

00:10:27.833 --> 00:10:30.666
Which again, feels weird
since Uzi didn't die here.

00:10:30.683 --> 00:10:33.566
How could the Absolute Solver program
have activated inside of her

00:10:33.566 --> 00:10:36.466
if she needs to have been a zombie bot
for it to have activated?

00:10:36.533 --> 00:10:39.583
Well, remember the code for these drones
functions a lot like DNA, meaning

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:42.100
that their programing can be handed down
between generations.

00:10:42.116 --> 00:10:45.466
It seems that Nori, the mother of Uzi,
passed it down to her daughter in the same way

00:10:45.466 --> 00:10:48.833
that human parents pass down hair color
or some other disease or trait.

00:10:48.833 --> 00:10:53.366
And when passed down this way, Absolute
Solver doesn't require death to activate.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:54.500
So Uzi teams up

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:57.900
with the disassembly drones to solve
the mystery of what's actually going on.

00:10:57.916 --> 00:10:59.066
Looking for clues around Copper

00:10:59.066 --> 00:11:03.016
9 eventually leads the gang to a place
called Cabin Fever Labs, an old J.C.

00:11:03.016 --> 00:11:03.983
Jenson facility.

00:11:03.983 --> 00:11:06.300
This place is filled
with a lot of interesting details.

00:11:06.316 --> 00:11:08.400
For one, the show makes a canon
that despite killing

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:12.533
all humans on Earth, the Doggos are,
in fact alive, safe and immortal.

00:11:12.533 --> 00:11:14.916
See, sometimes a plot hole
can be a great thing.

00:11:14.933 --> 00:11:18.016
Also at this place, we get ourselves
a tiny hint about a new character.

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:20.000
As Uzi’s exploring the Cabin Fever labs,

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:23.183
she picks up some paperwork that talks
about decommissioning a particular worker

00:11:23.183 --> 00:11:26.816
drone named S-010011X01.

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:28.350
We don't get an official name
for the drone,

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:29.250
but based on the way that this

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:33.183
series turns serial numbers and names,
the fans have dubbed this character S.

00:11:33.183 --> 00:11:36.300
You could argue that this is just a random
sheet of paper, but I don't think so.

00:11:36.316 --> 00:11:38.333
This clue about an S drone happens

00:11:38.333 --> 00:11:41.566
in the same episode where we learn
about the insane things that Uzi’s mom

00:11:41.566 --> 00:11:44.266
used to say before
she went full Absolute Solver.

00:11:52.216 --> 00:11:55.116
Sky demons, Singularity, cool ‘s’.

00:11:55.133 --> 00:11:56.266
More S things.

00:11:56.266 --> 00:11:58.550
I believe this S character
will be making their appearance

00:11:58.550 --> 00:12:01.816
sometime in the seventh
episode of the series, as a good robot

00:12:01.816 --> 00:12:05.333
built by Tessa, to make some big reveal
about Uzi’s mom's death.

00:12:05.333 --> 00:12:09.300
You see, in this series every drone has
two numbers that ultimately identify them.

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:11.416
One is their S/N or serial number,

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:13.483
a string of letters and numbers
unique to each character.

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:16.916
The second number is the P/N,
presumably the product number.

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:19.683
This string indicates
what model of drone they are.

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:21.983
Drones that are of the same kind
have the same P/Ns.

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:25.100
We see that this is true with N
and J and presumably V,

00:12:25.133 --> 00:12:28.616
who all have themselves
the same P/N, CYN-MYKX.

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:31.350
Notice that CYN or Cyn hidden right there.

00:12:31.366 --> 00:12:34.333
All the murder drones are part of the same
CYN model group

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:37.316
and have been controlled by Cyn
at some point throughout the series.

00:12:37.316 --> 00:12:41.066
However, this S has itself a completely
new P/N that we haven't seen before.

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:43.366
TSA-MMAC.

00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:47.233
Now the TSA there can't be a coincidence
when we have ourselves a human named Tessa

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:48.833
running around who loves repairing

00:12:48.833 --> 00:12:52.433
robots and is trying to stop Cyn’s
quest of absolute domination.

00:12:52.466 --> 00:12:53.716
In addition, I believe we'll discover

00:12:53.716 --> 00:12:56.783
that S was a friend or colleague of Uzi’s
mother Nori.

00:12:56.783 --> 00:12:57.316
How can I be sure?

00:12:57.316 --> 00:12:59.666
Well, in episode three, we're shown Nori’s
security badge,

00:12:59.666 --> 00:13:02.033
which shows that she used to work at Cabin
Fever Labs.

00:13:02.066 --> 00:13:05.633
The same is true for Yeva, another carrier
of the Absolute Solver corruption.

00:13:05.633 --> 00:13:06.566
And, wouldn't you know it?,

00:13:06.566 --> 00:13:10.166
but Cabin Fever Labs is the same place
that Uzi found the document about S.

00:13:10.166 --> 00:13:12.183
Furthermore,
take a closer look at that document.

00:13:12.183 --> 00:13:14.616
We see black creepy drawings
written all over it.

00:13:14.650 --> 00:13:16.450
These drawings bear a striking resemblance

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:18.533
to the ones that were drawn by Uzi’s
mother.

00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:20.800
So I think at this point it's
far more likely

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:24.400
that whoever this S character is
will be an old ally of Nori and Yeva

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:26.466
and will be willing to lend a hand
to Uzi and company

00:13:26.466 --> 00:13:28.916
as they try to defeat the Absolute
Solver corruption.

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:31.950
Moving back into the main story, though,
for a minute, after exploring around Cabin

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:35.283
Fever Labs, Uzi starts to act strange
and we begin to see the full extent

00:13:35.283 --> 00:13:36.866
of her Absolute Solver powers.

00:13:36.866 --> 00:13:39.866
She transforms into some kind of monster
and attacks all the other drones.

00:13:39.866 --> 00:13:41.216
After being talked down by N

00:13:41.216 --> 00:13:44.616
Uzi returns to normal, solidifying them
as the cutest couple ever.

00:13:44.650 --> 00:13:46.233
Eventually,
the team finds a key in the form

00:13:46.233 --> 00:13:50.050
of a robotic cricket that unlocks
the innermost part of Cabin Fever Labs.

00:13:50.066 --> 00:13:52.566
They then run into Tessa Elliot,
the girl who befriended

00:13:52.566 --> 00:13:54.416
Cyn and the other drones back in the day.

00:13:54.416 --> 00:13:57.150
Tessa, now an adult, has made it
her mission to stop Cyn

00:13:57.150 --> 00:14:00.216
and any other Absolute Solver drones
from taking over the universe.

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:02.466
In the end of the most recent episode,
we see Uzi, N and

00:14:02.466 --> 00:14:06.416
Tessa finding their way to the elevator
that leads down into the innermost lab,

00:14:06.416 --> 00:14:10.400
leaving them and us, the audience, curious
about what they'll discover down there.

00:14:10.416 --> 00:14:11.850
And honestly,
that's where the series stands

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:14.133
at the end of Episode six,
the creators of the show have said

00:14:14.133 --> 00:14:16.350
that the first season
will consist of eight episodes.

00:14:16.383 --> 00:14:17.916
We're really close to the end

00:14:17.916 --> 00:14:20.216
and there's still a lot of mysteries
that have yet to be solved.

00:14:20.216 --> 00:14:22.000
Is Uzi going to fully transform into Cyn?

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:24.366
Is this mysterious S character
going to make an appearance?

00:14:24.366 --> 00:14:26.766
Will Tessa be forced to kill
Uzi to save the world?

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:27.666
Well, loyal theorists,

00:14:27.666 --> 00:14:28.533
it turns out you don't

00:14:28.533 --> 00:14:31.700
have to wait for the next 4 to 6 months
for the final episodes to come out.

00:14:31.716 --> 00:14:34.533
I suspect that I already know everything
that's going to happen

00:14:34.533 --> 00:14:37.150
there, beat for beat, in the final two
episodes.

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:39.866
In fact, I suspect that we've known
where the show was headed

00:14:39.866 --> 00:14:43.616
since the very first scene
of the very first episode.

00:14:43.616 --> 00:14:44.316
Right after the

00:14:44.316 --> 00:14:48.033
opening exposition of episode one,
the show reveals everything to us.

00:14:48.066 --> 00:14:49.200
Right when we're first introduced.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:51.483
Uzi, she's giving a PowerPoint
presentation to her class

00:14:51.483 --> 00:14:52.750
about the dangers of sitting around

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:55.200
waiting for the murder drones
to find a way past the doors.

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:03.300
As she's talking,
this diagram appears behind her.

00:15:03.333 --> 00:15:06.783
This image is almost a complete rip
of the infamous hero's journey.

00:15:06.816 --> 00:15:08.350
If you're not familiar with that,
the hero's

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:09.866
journey is a storytelling template

00:15:09.866 --> 00:15:12.183
that many of the stories
throughout history have followed,

00:15:12.183 --> 00:15:13.683
from ancient epics like Gilgamesh

00:15:13.683 --> 00:15:16.566
and The Odyssey to modern classics
like Harry Potter and Star Wars.

00:15:16.566 --> 00:15:18.633
If you're writing yourself
a fantastical story,

00:15:18.633 --> 00:15:22.150
then the hero's journey is breaking down
the format of your narrative.

00:15:22.166 --> 00:15:25.250
Now, on my first viewing, I saw this
reference as just that, a reference.

00:15:25.266 --> 00:15:27.650
Murder Drones
loves meta humor and fourth wall breaks,

00:15:27.666 --> 00:15:29.616
so I just chalked this up to another
inside joke.

00:15:29.616 --> 00:15:31.950
However,
when I went back to the series again

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:34.950
in preparation for this theory, I realized
that everything that's happened in

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:39.033
the first six episodes has lined up
exactly with this little visual gag

00:15:39.066 --> 00:15:40.050
beat for beat.

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:42.866
We start off with the call to action
that draws the hero into adventure.

00:15:42.866 --> 00:15:44.883
Uzi wants to leave the safety of the doors

00:15:44.883 --> 00:15:46.750
and deal with the murder drones directly.

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:49.200
Then she gets saved by an unlikely aide, N.

00:15:49.233 --> 00:15:50.133
Right after this, Uzi

00:15:50.133 --> 00:15:53.383
teams up with a helper, Doll, and a mentor
who's been around the block, Tessa.

00:15:53.433 --> 00:15:55.750
Together, this crew fights back
against a bunch of challenges,

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:58.833
and Uzi becomes more and more tempted
to use the Absolute Solver

00:15:58.833 --> 00:16:00.300
abilities, culminating with her

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:04.616
being fully possessed by Cyn and N
having to cut off her hand in Episode six.

00:16:04.616 --> 00:16:08.400
And, mind you, this diagram
isn't just for any generic hero's journey.

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:11.050
It is one
that is specifically built for Uzi.

00:16:11.066 --> 00:16:14.166
Notice all the references
to angst moments built into this wheel,

00:16:14.166 --> 00:16:17.449
and if there is one emotion that definitely connects back to Uzi, it is angst.

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:26.550
So if I'm right, that then leaves us
right here on the chart

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:29.400
at the bottom, ready for another helper
and a big revelation

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:32.650
that's tied to three specific things:
rebirth, abyss and death.

00:16:32.666 --> 00:16:34.766
So remind me again
where these characters are:

00:16:34.766 --> 00:16:38.016
exploring Cabin Fever
Labs, a giant underground facility

00:16:38.016 --> 00:16:42.366
that keeps going down to the center
point, an abyss, if you will.

00:16:42.366 --> 00:16:44.666
And who have we been talking about
this entire time:

00:16:44.666 --> 00:16:47.716
S, potential new helper
associated with Uzi’s dead

00:16:47.716 --> 00:16:50.816
mom, who probably has more information
than anyone about what happened to her.

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:52.716
According to the hero's journey
at this point, Uzi,

00:16:52.716 --> 00:16:55.116
our hero, will be given
some sort of a revelation.

00:16:55.116 --> 00:16:57.033
S is going to reveal some saucy detail,

00:16:57.033 --> 00:17:01.283
something that, according to our template,
will lead Uzi to a full angst overload.

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:04.016
If what we've seen
previously are just tiny overloads,

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:05.766
what could this possibly look like?

00:17:05.766 --> 00:17:07.950
What could be so devastating for
Uzi to hear

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:10.166
that it’ll
send her into a presumed meltdown?

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:13.916
Well, I suspect that S is going to reveal
that N was the murder drone

00:17:13.916 --> 00:17:15.783
who killed Uzi’s mom. Yeah.

00:17:15.783 --> 00:17:18.750
I'm just as shocked as you are,
but N is the most likely killer.

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:21.216
At this point in the series we don't
actually know what happened to Nori.

00:17:21.233 --> 00:17:24.216
All we know was
that she was bitten by some murder drone

00:17:24.216 --> 00:17:26.583
and that Nori's father
ultimately had to put her down.

00:17:26.583 --> 00:17:30.800
On Copper 9 there's only three murder drones
that we know of: V, J and, of course, N.

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:33.750
Nothing would make
for a more catastrophic revelation

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:36.966
than finding out your new boyfriend
is the one that killed your mom.

00:17:36.983 --> 00:17:40.616
This devastating news will be too much for
Uzi to handle, and she'll fully succumb

00:17:40.616 --> 00:17:44.100
to her Absolute Solver powers
going full angst overload.

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:46.466
Then, after being saved once again
by N in the crew,

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:49.900
Uzi will atone for her sins
and make her mission for season two clear:

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:53.516
defeat Cyn, stop the Absolute Solver,
and end the cycle of violence

00:17:53.516 --> 00:17:56.766
that prevents humans and drones
from living the peaceful lives they want.

00:17:56.800 --> 00:17:59.133
So, there you have it,
my friends, Murder Drones, the plot

00:17:59.133 --> 00:18:02.250
so far as well as a solid hypothesis
or two on where it might be headed

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:04.883
in the final episodes of the season.
How am I so sure about this?

00:18:04.883 --> 00:18:06.566
I guess you could say
it's a mixture of Easter eggs,

00:18:06.566 --> 00:18:09.566
narrative logic and a whole lot
of watching other forms of media.

00:18:09.566 --> 00:18:12.133
You know, stuff like movies, TV
shows and of course, anime.

00:18:15.366 --> 00:18:16.350
But hey!

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:19.983
That's just a theory. A FILM THEORY!

00:18:20.016 --> 00:18:21.883
aaaaaaand cut.

